One important upgrade from the first curriculum

I found something especially relevant while checking current instruction-following research.

Modern instruction-following evaluation is moving toward verifiable instructions and programmatic verification, rather than relying entirely on subjective judgment. IFEval was designed around verifiable instruction types, while 2026 work such as LexInstructEval explicitly decomposes instructions into structured components and uses transparent programmatic verification. 

So I'm adding a principle to our course:

> Whenever possible, convert an instruction into something whose compliance can be independently checked.



Instead of:

> "Be careful."



Use:

> "Before execution, record the SHA-256 hash. After execution, calculate it again. The hashes MUST match."



The second isn't merely more detailed.

It is verifiable.


